  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Much colder air persists tonight into early next \nweek. A Freeze Warning is in effect for portions of the area tonight \ninto Saturday morning, with near-freezing temperatures possible \nagain Sunday morning. A Frost Advisory has been issued along the \neastern side of the Freeze Warning tonight.\n\nA deep upper low is noted over the NE CONUS with a strengthening \nsurface low off of the NJ coast. Cold temperatures aloft, associated \nwith the upper low, have allowed for scattered to broken cloud cover \nover much of the region. NW winds remain breezy this afternoon with \ndecent mixing. Winds will decrease this evening and overnight, \nespecially away from the coast. Water vapor imagery shows a short \nwave trough to the west and southwest of the local area. This \ndisturbance will translate across the region late this afternoon \ninto the evening, bringing enhanced lift and a chance for showers, \nmainly near and south of the VA/NC border. A few showers are also \npossible across the Eastern Shore this evening. Any light precip \nshould come to an end around midnight with clouds expected to \ndecrease toward sunrise in the wake of the upper trough. \n\nThe Freeze Warning is in effect from late tonight through 8AM \nSaturday. A Frost Advisory has been issued from the Northern Neck \nsouthward along the eastern flank of the Freeze Warning. Blended \nguidance continues below most of the global and statistical \ntemperature guidance. Questions remain regarding exactly when clouds \nthin out and winds decrease, leading to some uncertainty as to just \nhow cold we will be able to get tonight/early Saturday. The forecast \nhas widespread upper 20s to low 30s for most of the inland area with \nmid to upper 30s expected near the coast and into eastern NE NC. \nTemperatures for the MD Eastern Shore will be tricky with more wind \nand clouds expected. Have not included the Eastern Shore in any of \nthe frost/freeze headlines but interior portions of Dorchester and \nWicomico could see some freezing temps if winds decrease and \nclouds are not as extensive as currently forecast. Dry and \ncooler weather will continue Saturday and well into next week \nwith highs in the 50s to low 60s and lows in the upper 20s to \nlow 30s inland (mid 30s to low 40s along the coast). Will wait \nto see how observations go tonight before addressing the \nfrost/freeze potential on subsequent nights. It appears that \ntomorrow night into early next week will see more classically \nfavorable radiational cooling conditions with clear skies and \nlighter winds expected with high pressure moving over the\nregion.",
